\ Move from the chosen year 5000 in either calendar direction.
\ NEXT uses seal 11 and the old closing gate as its opening gate.
\ PREVIOUS uses seal 12 and the old opening gate as its closing gate.
\ The sauce pair is (original calculation day, boundary gate day).
\ Every future or previous year begins or ends exactly at that boundary.
\ Candidate years have at least six intervals and 252..5778 days.
\ Candidate ordering remains shortest first, then earlier opening gate.
\ The original calculation day never changes while walking across years.
\ This code is pending a genuine CAL compiler test.

The transition boundary is a calendar day.
The transition seal is a number.
The current year number is a number.
The target year day is a calendar day.

\ Only the directionally eligible side is scanned. Since every new gap
\ is positive, elapsed year duration increases monotonically; therefore
\ candidates are appended directly in canonical shortest-first order.
To gather following year candidates:
  Destroy the eligible year ledger.
  Put 0 into the eligible year count.
  Put the chosen year's closing day into the transition boundary.
  Put 11 into the transition seal.
  Put the chosen year's closing index into the gate walk's current index.
  Put the transition boundary into the gate walk's current position.
  Loop.
    Move to the next positive gate.
    Put the transition boundary into the candidate under construction's opening day.
    Put the chosen year's closing index into the candidate under construction's opening index.
    Put the gate walk's current position into the candidate under construction's closing day.
    Put the gate walk's current index into the candidate under construction's closing index.
    Measure the candidate under construction.
    If the candidate under construction's year length is greater than 5778, break.
    If the candidate under construction's span count is less than 6, repeat.
    Add one following candidate.
  Repeat.

To add one following candidate:
  Create a year option.
  Put the candidate under construction into the year option's year candidate.
  Append the year option to the eligible year ledger.
  Add 1 to the eligible year count.

\ Scanning backward also encounters shortest durations first; equal
\ durations cannot arise when one boundary gate is held fixed.
To gather preceding year candidates:
  Destroy the eligible year ledger.
  Put 0 into the eligible year count.
  Put the chosen year's opening day into the transition boundary.
  Put 12 into the transition seal.
  Put the chosen year's opening index into the gate walk's current index.
  Put the transition boundary into the gate walk's current position.
  Loop.
    Move to the next negative gate.
    Put the gate walk's current position into the candidate under construction's opening day.
    Put the gate walk's current index into the candidate under construction's opening index.
    Put the transition boundary into the candidate under construction's closing day.
    Put the chosen year's opening index into the candidate under construction's closing index.
    Measure the candidate under construction.
    If the candidate under construction's year length is greater than 5778, break.
    If the candidate under construction's span count is less than 6, repeat.
    Add one following candidate.
  Repeat.

\ This selection uses c from year selection day and the fixed boundary,
\ not the pair that was used for a preceding gate gap.
To select the adjacent year:
  Put the year selection day into the example's calculation day.
  Put the transition boundary into the example's requested day.
  Answer the example.
  Copy the example into the sauce input.
  Make the complete sauce.
  Put 1 into the seal inquiry's bowl identity.
  Put the transition seal into the seal inquiry's seal number.
  Begin a sealed inquiry.
  Convert the eligible year count to a string.
  Put the string into a transition count wide quantity.
  Choose an equal option given the transition count wide quantity.
  Convert the seal inquiry's selected quantity to the selected year rank.
  Read the selected year candidate.

\ A failed scan or invalid rank must never overwrite the last successfully selected year.
To advance to the following year:
  Put 0 into the year selection success.
  Gather following year candidates.
  If the eligible year count is 0,
    destroy the eligible year ledger; exit.
  Select the adjacent year.
  If the selected year available is not 1,
    destroy the eligible year ledger; exit.
  Add 1 to the current year number.
  Put 1 into the year selection success.
  Destroy the eligible year ledger.

To move to the preceding year:
  Put 0 into the year selection success.
  Gather preceding year candidates.
  If the eligible year count is 0,
    destroy the eligible year ledger; exit.
  Select the adjacent year.
  If the selected year available is not 1,
    destroy the eligible year ledger; exit.
  Subtract 1 from the current year number.
  Put 1 into the year selection success.
  Destroy the eligible year ledger.

\ Forward and backward movement use different routines so CAL sees only
\ one loop in each routine.
To travel forward to the target day:
  Loop.
    If the target year day is not greater than the chosen year's closing day, break.
    Advance to the following year.
    If the year selection success is not 1, exit.
  Repeat.

To travel backward to the target day:
  Loop.
    If the target year day is greater than the chosen year's opening day, break.
    Move to the preceding year.
    If the year selection success is not 1, exit.
  Repeat.

\ First argument is the calculation day; second is the requested day.
\ The complete date has not yet been resolved into cutlets and months.
To find the year for a calendar day and another calendar day:
  Put the other calendar day into the target year day.
  Find year 5000 for the calendar day.
  If the year selection success is not 1, exit.
  Put 5000 into the current year number.
  If the target year day is greater than the chosen year's closing day,
    travel forward to the target day; exit.
  If the target year day is less than or equal to the chosen year's opening day,
    travel backward to the target day; exit.
